g’s “Metropolis” airs alongside one of its forebears.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/02/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday-the-good-the-bad-the-hungry-and-metropolis.html', 'snippet': 'A documentary looks at a rivalry between competitive eaters. And Fritz Lang’s “Metropolis” airs alongside one of its forebears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE GOOD, THE BAD, THE HUNGRY 8 p.m. on ESPN. A warning to viewers: think twice before using this documentary as inspiration for your July 4 cookouts. The latest entry in ESPN’s “30 for 30” series focuses on the rivalry between the competitive eaters Takeru Kobayashi and Joey Chestnut, who was a 23-year-old engineering student from California when he defeated Kobayashi at the Nathan’s Famous Hot Dog Eating Contest in 2007. To do that, Chestnut ate 66 hot dogs in 12 minutes. “Like a particularly bloody boxing match, it was disturbing to watch,” Ken Belson wrote in The New York Times. Not all will have the stomach for the repeated footage of franks and buns disappearing into the men’s gullets like water down a drain, but those who do should enjoy the semi-serious, high-stakes tone this documentary strikes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/02/arts/02tvcol-goodbadhungry/02tvcol-goodbadhungry-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/02/arts/02tvcol-goodbadhungry/02tvcol-goodbadhungry-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/02/arts/02tvcol-goodbadhungry/02tvcol-goodbadhungry-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im