 None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ea45c53-9fa0-5f8f-80d8-e9e8f1f871d4', 'word_count': 1608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ea45c53-9fa0-5f8f-80d8-e9e8f1f871d4'}, {'abstract': 'Break down the characters into chunks and repeat them over and over. Write them down ASAP, even if only in the dirt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/02/magazine/how-to-remember-a-license-plate.html', 'snippet': 'Break down the characters into chunks and repeat them over and over. Write them down ASAP, even if only in the dirt.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Memory decays very quickly,” says Caren Rotello, chairwoman of the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst. In studies about license-plate recall, people who see a plate for three or fewer seconds remember an average of around 3.5 characters (and fewer when seen reversed in a rearview mirror). After that, the more time that elapses, the more you’ll forget. “Write it down immediately,” Rotello says. “Even if you just take a stick or your finger and write in the dirt.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07Mag-Tip-1/07Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07Mag-Tip-1/07Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07Mag-Tip-1/07Mag-Tip-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07Mag-Tip-1/07Mag-Tip-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1465, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJ