_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c348f5b8-37fe-554c-b4e3-96183848e1fb', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c348f5b8-37fe-554c-b4e3-96183848e1fb'}, {'abstract': 'A docuseries about the Romanov family premieres on Netflix. And a film about one of the bloodiest incidents in British history comes to Amazon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/03/arts/whats-on-tv-wednesday-the-last-czars-and-peterloo.html', 'snippet': 'A docuseries about the Romanov family premieres on Netflix. And a film about one of the bloodiest incidents in British history comes to Amazon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OPHELIA (2018) Stream on IFC Films. Imagine if Shakespeare’s “Hamlet” were told from Ophelia’s perspective. This film, directed by Claire McCarthy, follows Ophelia (Daisey Ridley), a Danish orphan who becomes a lady in waiting to Queen Gertrude (Naomi Watts). Soon she and a young Prince Hamlet (George MacKay) fall in love. But Ophelia’s devotion is tested as Hamlet’s desire to avenge his father’s death overtakes him. In her review in The New York Times, Manohla Dargis wrote: “‘Ophelia’ seeks to revamp the image of its title heroine as a tragic, largely passive casualty, one who is as much a victim of Shakespeare’s era as of his peerless imagination. It’s an interesting exercise and, for the most part, a passably diverting one.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/03/arts/03tvcol-ophelia/merlin_156820329_fc676dc6-fc80-4114-b6f5-e66241400204-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/03/arts/03tvcol-ophelia/merlin_156820329_fc676dc6-fc80-4114-b6f5-e66241400204-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/