, {'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e37587ab-316f-53d3-b421-27fae1cffcb2', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e37587ab-316f-53d3-b421-27fae1cffcb2'}, {'abstract': 'Dinghies are a means to salvation, not just from a shipwreck, but as an escape from the catastrophes of life — including one’s own family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/03/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-dinghy-rowing.html', 'snippet': 'Dinghies are a means to salvation, not just from a shipwreck, but as an escape from the catastrophes of life — including one’s own family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first learned to row because I wanted to escape the people I loved. My family spent summer weekends on a semi-rotted wooden sailboat. It was basically a floating camper van we drove up and down the coast of Maine, except that we couldn’t easily leave it when we stopped for the night. If one of us wanted space or privacy, we got into the dinghy — a smaller boat we towed off the stern — and rowed to an uninhabited island to stare at rocks and reset our mood. Our boat was named Second Chance by its previous owners; we memorialized our need for the occasional sanity-restoring break from one another by naming our dinghy Last Chance.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07mag-LOR/31d9de18605c41b3aed51f615e865952-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07mag-LOR/31d9de18605c41b3aed51f615e865952-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'