 just as often as the usual game highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kevin Durant’s defining quality, as arguably the world’s greatest basketball player, has always been his ease. He dominates other pros like a grown uncle bossing the mini-hoop at his nephew’s backyard birthday party. Type Durant’s name into a search bar, and you can binge for hours on mesmerizing footage of him gently devastating his peers. He blocks an opponent’s shot while wearing only one shoe. He bounds through crowds of defenders with two giant steps, then dunks almost as an afterthought. He makes impossible last-second game-winning shots as casually as the rest of us might toss our car keys into a bowl on the coffee table. Once, using the subtlest of shot fakes, Durant sent LeBron James sliding helplessly to the floor, out of bounds, and then cruised politely forward for two points. Sometimes this ease can work against him: Durant’s dominance looks anticlimactic, and his highlights hardly even scan as highlights. He’s like a superhero with too many super\u200bpowers. Of course he wins in the end.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07mag-screenland-image1/0fb2afd2cc8a4605b91d3ecd829f8102-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07mag-screenland-image1/0fb2afd2cc8a4605b91d3ecd829f8102-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/magazine/07mag-screenland-image1/0fb2afd2cc8a4605b91d3ecd829f8102-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/0