 {'abstract': 'Roger Federer and Serena Williams are in action, but many eyes will be on Rafael Nadal and Nick Kyrgios.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/04/sports/tennis/wimbledon-what-to-watch-day-4.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Federer and Serena Williams are in action, but many eyes will be on Rafael Nadal and Nick Kyrgios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month Nadal won his 12th title at Roland Garros, losing only two sets along the way. Although he has won just two Wimbledon titles, the third-seeded Nadal looks in fine form at majors this year, having also reached the final at the Australian Open. None of that will intimidate Kyrgios, the enigmatic player who can throw tantrums one minute and make subliminally difficult shots the next. Kyrgios has beaten Nadal three times, including in the fourth round at Wimbledon in 2014, when Nadal was the No. 1 seed. After Kyrgios defeated Nadal at the Acapulco Open in February, Nadal said he “lacks respect for the crowd, his opponent and towards himself.” Kyrgios lived up to this description in his first-round match, a five-set spectacle against Jordan Thompson, in which he alternated between joking with the crowd, admonishing himself for poor decision making, and trying to be conciliatory. Late Wednesday night, Kyrgios was even spotted by reporters at a Wimbledon Village bar. It’s hard to know which version of Kyrgios will show up Thursday, but, at the least, you can expect a match full of scorching forehands and high emotions under the watchful eye of the Centre Court crowd.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/merlin_157346292_a643e385-b58f-42ee-be87-3d1716b51705-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/merlin_157346292_a643e385-b58f-42ee-be87-3d1716b51705-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/merlin_157346292_a643e385-b58f-42ee-be87-3d1716b51705-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/merlin_157346292_a643e385-b58f-42ee-be87-3d1716b51705-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1454, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/04tennis-watch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/04tennis-watch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/sports/04tennis-watch/04tennis-watch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What to Watch Thursday at Wimbledon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Andy (1987- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Konta, Johanna (1991- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siniakova, Katerina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kyrgios, Nick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barty, Ashleigh', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-04T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Gendler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gendler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79e9f15e-a423-5ffb-840f-6291ee473a73', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79e9f15e-a423-5ffb-840f-6291ee473a73'}, {'abstract': '“Stranger Things” returns for a third season on Netflix. And Macy’s Fourth of July celebration broadcasts from New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/04/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday-stranger-things-and-fireworks.html', 'snippet': '“Stranger Things” returns for a third season on Netflix. And Macy’s Fourth of July celebration broadcasts from New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STRANGER THINGS Stream on Netflix. After a bit over a year and a half off, the 1980s are back. Or at least the ’80s as shown through the Spielbergian lens of this Netflix sci-fi horror series, which follows a group of Midwestern youths coming-of-age in a town that exists in the shadow of the supernatural. Season 2 ramped up the emotion and romance amid the chaos. Season 3 finds the friends in the summer between middle school and high school — so it’ll probably be downright terrifying. (In an interview with The New York Times, the actor Cary Elwes, who plays the town mayor this season, mentioned “Fast Times at Ridgemont High” as a primary influence for Season 3.) The neon nostalgia factor runs high.  “I always wonder if Apple iPhones and all these electronics that we use today are still going to be a thing in like 10, 20 years,” the actor Noah Schnapp, who plays one of the friends, recently told The Times. “It’s nice being on set, because it teaches you, oh, you don’t need your phone all the time. People lived like this.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/04/arts/04tvcol-strangerthings/04tvcol-strangerthings-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Thursday: ‘Stranger Things’ and Fireworks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-04T05:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gabe Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37b26844-edfc-5ed8-8c2a-b75ac12799ba', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37b26844-edfc-5ed8-8c2a-b75ac12799ba'}, {'abstract': 'The Polish-American architect Daniel Libeskind was asked to design an exhibit at the death camp site about faith, and to engage without distracting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/04/world/europe/auschwitz-daniel-libeskind.html', 'snippet': 'The Polish-American architect Daniel Libeskind was asked to design an exhibit at the death camp site about faith, and to engage without distracting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OSWIECIM, Poland — The letter to Daniel Libeskind’s father arrived shortly after the war ended. His sister, Rozia, informed him that his family was dead. She was the only one of 10 siblings to survive Auschwitz. Over three handwritten pages in Yiddish, she detailed the horrors they endured.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/02/world/02Auschwitz1/merlin_157345419_a8d1f521-47da-42de-a766-c19e975205c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/02/world/02Auschwitz1/merlin_157345419_a8d1f521-47da-42de-a766-c19e975205c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 