ion_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/761cc519-f8a4-58bb-a838-fd2a40e4cac2', 'word_count': 1481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/761cc519-f8a4-58bb-a838-fd2a40e4cac2'}, {'abstract': 'Sterling Road is newly hip, thanks to the arrival of the Museum of Contemporary Art. But with its elevated profile, locals worry the old strip will lose its gritty magic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/05/travel/sterling-road-toronto-museum-of-contemporary-art.html', 'snippet': 'Sterling Road is newly hip, thanks to the arrival of the Museum of Contemporary Art. But with its elevated profile, locals worry the old strip will lose its gritty magic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dismissed for decades as a postindustrial wasteland, Sterling Road, a zigzagging half-mile strip of old factories and warehouses, is getting a second life. Last summer, the North American debut of a splashy Banksy exhibition in an empty warehouse there drew a global spotlight. With the arrival of Toronto’s Museum of Contemporary Art (MOCA) last fall, Sterling Road is newly hip, its appeal broadening beyond the small cadre of tuned-in artists and bohemian types who for years have had it to themselves. The street’s cavernous structures have also quickly become hot real estate.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/07/travel/05Toronto-HeadsUp2/merlin_156064710_b6c26553-b307-4f85-b4fa-9cfc425e5f72-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/07/travel/05Toronto-HeadsUp2/merlin_156064710_b6c26553-b307-4f85-b4f