com/2019/07/08/science/animal-fingerprints-stripes-patterns.html', 'snippet': 'In some species, individual animals can be distinguished by unique identifiers, not unlike human fingerprints.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A. Scientists generally agree that closely related species like gorillas, chimpanzees and orangutans have ridge patterns on fingers and toes that resemble those of humans and can be used to identify individuals. As with humans, no two individuals with the same prints have been found.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1441, 'width': 1471, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/09/science/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo/Sci-QA-AnimalPrints-illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}