anization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c32eb055-5228-5885-9f49-d0281793a1c4', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c32eb055-5228-5885-9f49-d0281793a1c4'}, {'abstract': 'Despite mounting evidence that elephants find captivity torturous, some American zoos still acquire them from Africa — aided by a tall tale about why they needed to leave home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/09/magazine/elephants-zoos-swazi-17.html', 'snippet': 'Despite mounting evidence that elephants find captivity torturous, some American zoos still acquire them from Africa — aided by a tall tale about why they needed to leave home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Elephants of the Zambezi River Valley” enclosure at the Sedgwick County Zoo in Wichita, Kan., is a dreamscape idyll of an elephant’s natural home: five and a half sprawling acres of tree-dotted mock savanna and a 550,000-gallon pond where boated people and wading pachyderms can nearly meet on opposite sides of a discreetly submerged barrier. All eight of the zoo’s elephants were visible when I visited on Memorial Day 2018, two years after the habitat’s grand opening, including six recent arrivals from the tiny southern African kingdom eSwatini (formerly Swaziland), the lot of them moving about with the same slow, tensile synchrony of larger wild elephant herds. Only the background flicker of cars on Interstate 235 disrupted the tableau, as well as my own occasional thoughts of far less accommodated zoo and circus elephant captives over the years, right back to the very first elephant brought to the United States.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/3fd01892d37e4b1481015abfe9e9bb78-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/3fd01892d37e4b1481015abfe9e9bb78-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/3fd01892d37e4b1481015abfe9e9bb78-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/3fd01892d37e4b1481015abfe9e9bb78-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/14mag-zoos-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/14mag-zoos-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/magazine/14mag-zoos-02/14mag-zoos-02-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Zoos Called It a ‘Rescue.’ But Are the Elephants Really Better Off?', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Swazi 17', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elephants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Swaziland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Abuse, Rights and Welfare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Behavior', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Zoo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Zoo', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-09T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Siebert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siebert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/327d8d92-f4fd-543b-8c06-c2915b15c614', 'word_count': 7278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/327d8d92-f4fd-543b-8c06-c2915b15c614'}, {'abstract': 'The surprise winner of last year’s Leadville 100 ultramarathon credits the performance-enhancing power of parenthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/09/well/move/leadville-katie-arnold-ultramarathon-training-parenthood.html', 'snippet': 'The surprise winner of last year’s Leadville 100 ultramarathon credits the performance-enhancing power of parenthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mention to people you’re training for a 50- or 60-mile ultramarathon and the list of questions they’ll ask will be nearly as long as the race itself. “What do you think about when you run?” they wonder. “Where do you pee? When do you sleep? How do you do it all?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/02/well/00arnold-leadville/00arnold-leadville-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/02/well/00arnold-leadville/00arnold-leadville-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/02/well/00arnold-leadville/00arnold-leadville-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width':