riginal': 'By Margot Sanger-Katz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanger-Katz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56dea47a-8eab-5038-9cbc-359ac30fa639', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56dea47a-8eab-5038-9cbc-359ac30fa639'}, {'abstract': 'In their rush to match Netflix, competitors like HBO, Hulu and Amazon are ordering a slew of content — ushering out the age of “prestige TV” and ushering in an age of anything goes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/10/magazine/streaming-race-netflix-hbo-hulu-amazon.html', 'snippet': 'In their rush to match Netflix, competitors like HBO, Hulu and Amazon are ordering a slew of content — ushering out the age of “prestige TV” and ushering in an age of anything goes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Nick Weidenfeld heard what happened at HBO last summer, he was thrilled. “Everyone I knew was texting that article around, saying, ‘What the [expletive]!’\u2009” Weidenfeld, an independent TV producer, recently recalled. A lot of people who work in Hollywood were spooked by the news, but not him: “I thought it was amazing.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great Race to Rule Streaming TV', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stream of Rivals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box