egislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-10T15:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Warzel and Ash Ngu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warzel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ash', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ngu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/5fded685-df4d-5272-8eed-a12876f70c2e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/5fded685-df4d-5272-8eed-a12876f70c2e'}, {'abstract': 'Moises Velasquez-Manoff responds to people from the state who commented on the contradictory nature of race relations there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/10/opinion/hawaii-race-ethnicity.html', 'snippet': 'Moises Velasquez-Manoff responds to people from the state who commented on the contradictory nature of race relations there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my essay “Want to Be Less Racist? Move to Hawaii,” I explore why race might be perceived differently in Hawaii from how it is on the mainland United States. The article grew out of my interest in the psychologist Kristin Pauker’s research, which suggests that kids in Hawaii see race as something much more fluid than kids on the mainland do and that this fluidity can rub off on people from the mainland who enroll in college there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/30/opinion/sunday/30velasquez-manoff-6/merlin_156981249_6e1adee5-776c-44b7-8951-4ac81d1b1504-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/06/30/opinion/sunday/30velasquez-manoff-6/merlin_15698124