ame': 'persons', 'value': 'Tatishvili, Anna', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-11T20:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f481b28a-9ccd-5312-8693-024eb75d75f6', 'word_count': 1327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f481b28a-9ccd-5312-8693-024eb75d75f6'}, {'abstract': 'Through both his everyman path to professional baseball and his revealing memoir “Ball Four,” Bouton, who died on Wednesday, made a gilded world seem attainable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/11/sports/baseball/jim-bouton-death-career.html', 'snippet': 'Through both his everyman path to professional baseball and his revealing memoir “Ball Four,” Bouton, who died on Wednesday, made a gilded world seem attainable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In high school they called him Warm-Up, because he only ever pitched in the bullpen. He was an unknown, this Jim Bouton, having just moved to Illinois from New Jersey. He loved the game, sure. But that didn’t mean they had to let him pitch.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/sports/11keper-bouton4/merlin_157775550_2c6e1750-c46c-4362-94a9-e5c7de0817f6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/14/sports/11keper-bouton4/merlin_157775550_2c6e1750-c46c-4362-94a9-e5c7de0817f6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/14/sports/1