ountries', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Morning Edition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-15T04:12:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b84db58d-b09e-53c4-9507-94d3f2942061', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b84db58d-b09e-53c4-9507-94d3f2942061'}, {'abstract': 'Celeste goes through the wringer in court, as expected. But she’s got one final trick up her sleeve.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/15/arts/television/big-little-lies-episode-6-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Celeste goes through the wringer in court, as expected. But she’s got one final trick up her sleeve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you make a limited series television show and wrap its finale in a decent bow, but then rabid enthusiasm for the show leads you to make a second season — yet you feel pretty certain you absolutely will not make a third season — well, then the penultimate episode of the actual last season is the perfect place to drop a monster cliffhanger. And “Big Little Lies” brought it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/15/arts/15lies-recap/merlin_157808580_1cd9619a-b0db-498c-ba44-940e0a56d97b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/15/arts/15lies-recap/merlin_157808580_1cd9619a-b0db-498c-ba44-940e0a56d97b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/15/arts/15lies-recap/merlin_157808580_1cd