 'The twisty Netflix comedy received 13 Emmy nominations for its first season, but its star and creator is already thinking about Season 2.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the premise of “Russian Doll” sounded familiar — a woman lives through the same night of her life, over and over and over again, to her frustration and eventual enlightenment — the actual show was anything but. This Netflix comedy series, which stars Natasha Lyonne as Nadia, the protagonist caught in an inexplicable time loop, and which she created with Leslye Headland and Amy Poehler, was a heartfelt and idiosyncratic tribute to the uncertainties of middle age, to the East Village and to Lyonne’s own life, whose details are encrypted throughout the show. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/16/arts/16emmy-lyonne/16emmy-lyonne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Natasha Lyonne Is Thrilled For ‘Russian Doll’ and the Emmys, We Think', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lyonne, Natasha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Russian Doll (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-16T19:24:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c721741a-329b-5ce2-88c7-8626fef04231', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c721741a-329b-5ce2-88c7-8626fef04231'}, {'abstract': 'For decades, smaller metropolitan areas closed the income gap with bigger, richer ones, but no longer. So places like Winston-Salem, N.C., are trying to lay a new foundation for prosperity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/16/business/economy/winston-salem-convergence.html', 'snippet': 'For decades, smaller metropolitan areas closed the income gap with bigger, richer ones, but no longer. So places like Winston-Salem, N.C., are trying to lay a new foundation for prosperity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WINSTON-SALEM, N.C. — Within sight of a couple of huge brick smokestacks, looming witnesses to a past built on tobacco and powered by coal, there is something weirdly out of place a