arah Firshein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firshein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68ebe466-428b-5544-a04c-32164091996d', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68ebe466-428b-5544-a04c-32164091996d'}, {'abstract': 'For decades, films and television have seen a generation wax nostalgic about how it transformed America. The reality is something very different.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/17/magazine/bob-dylan-and-the-myth-of-boomer-idealism.html', 'snippet': 'For decades, films and television have seen a generation wax nostalgic about how it transformed America. The reality is something very different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his Netflix film “Rolling Thunder Revue,” which chronicles Bob Dylan’s face-paint-streaked 1975 tour of the same name, Martin Scorsese introduces us to a former congressman from Michigan named Jack Tanner. Tanner sits at a farmhouse table in a spacious home, wearing a tweed jacket and no tie, hair gray and a little shaggy, a patrician Everyboomer who could have walked out of a financial-planning ad. In November of ’75, he says, he was on the way home from a meeting with Jimmy Carter when a storm grounded his plane in Niagara Falls, N.Y. He called Carter from the motel. “He says, ‘Well, you just hit the jackpot,’\u2009” Tanner recalls. “\u2009‘Bob Dylan is doing this Rolling Thunder concert there tonight, and you can go.’\u2009”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/21/magazine/21mag-screenland-1/21mag-screenland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/21/magazine/21mag-screenland-1/21mag-screenland-1-articl