ganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5868f92-6c8f-5a66-bad0-0fb0058a58d3', 'word_count': 2081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5868f92-6c8f-5a66-bad0-0fb0058a58d3'}, {'abstract': 'The designer Rejina Pyo and the store owner Alex Eagle host an artful but laid-back dinner for friends, with a menu by Pyo’s husband, the chef Jordan Bourke.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/22/t-magazine/rejina-pyo-korean-dinner-party.html', 'snippet': 'The designer Rejina Pyo and the store owner Alex Eagle host an artful but laid-back dinner for friends, with a menu by Pyo’s husband, the chef Jordan Bourke.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The designer Rejina Pyo moved to London from Seoul, South Korea, in 2008 to study fashion at Central Saint Martins, under the esteemed professor Louise Wilson. Not long after, she met her husband, the chef and cookbook author Jordan Bourke — and then, much to her parents’ dismay, she never moved back home. After graduating, Pyo joined the team of the London-based designer Roksanda Ilincic — with whom she shares an affinity for deeply saturated colors (taupe, ocher, plum) and fluid, oversize cuts — before starting her own eponymous line in 2014. Now known for its easy, elegant pieces such as puff-sleeved cotton dresses, relaxed trench coats in laminated wool and asymmetrically cinched linen suits, the brand has become a fixture on the London Fashion Week schedule. (The birth of Pyo’s son, Luka, in 2017 only further entrenched her in England.) Nevertheless, after 11 years away from her hometown, the designer revisits her Korean roots on a nearly daily basis, through food.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/17/t-magazine/17tmag-pyo-slide-K562/17tmag-pyo-slide-K562-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_