edia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adnan Khan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adnan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/dbd620ac-a2fc-5593-b1b7-4abb87952ca0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/dbd620ac-a2fc-5593-b1b7-4abb87952ca0'}, {'abstract': 'Venezuela has a chance to start loosening the grip of harsh disunity by releasing all of its political prisoners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/22/opinion/maria-lourdes-afiuni-chomsky-venezuela.html', 'snippet': 'Venezuela has a chance to start loosening the grip of harsh disunity by releasing all of its political prisoners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'July 5, Venezuela’s Independence Day, came with a precious piece of news for all those who hope for the country’s future: Michele Bachelet, the United Nations High Commissioner for human rights, announced that the Venezuelan judge María Lourdes Afiuni had been released from parole, after almost 10 years of confinement. But her release came with conditions — the judge in charge of her case left in place restrictions that precluded her from traveling abroad and limited her freedom of speech. Judge Afiuni was liberated along with a journalist, Braulio Jatar, and 20 other political prisoners. Thus, one of Hugo Chávez’s most arbitrary acts has finally been reversed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/merlin_157517658_a8394574-d3e3-49f4-8e12-3aaa50946f08-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/merlin_157517658_a8394574-d3e3-49f4-8e12-3aaa50946f08-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/merlin_157517658_a8394574-d3e3-49f4-8e12-3aaa50946f08-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/merlin_157517658_a8394574-d3e3-49f4-8e12-3aaa50946f08-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1330, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/22chomskyWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/22chomskyWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/22/opinion/22chomskyWeb/22chomskyWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One of Chávez’s Most Arbitrary Acts Has Finally Been Reversed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Afiuni, Maria Lourdes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maduro, Nicolas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, 