ya Safronova', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valeriya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Safronova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76a5bb63-fcc1-58b3-9ab8-ccfab9ab6fe7', 'word_count': 3682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76a5bb63-fcc1-58b3-9ab8-ccfab9ab6fe7'}, {'abstract': 'Leonardo DiCaprio and Brad Pitt star as midlevel entertainment industry workers whose relationship forms the core of Quentin Tarantino’s look at the movie past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/24/movies/once-upon-a-time-in-hollywood-review.html', 'snippet': 'Leonardo DiCaprio and Brad Pitt star as midlevel entertainment industry workers whose relationship forms the core of Quentin Tarantino’s look at the movie past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a lot of love in “Once Upon a Time … in Hollywood,” and quite a bit to enjoy. The screen is crowded with signs of Quentin Tarantino’s well-established ardor — for the movies and television shows of the decades after World War II; for the vernacular architecture, commercial signage and famous restaurants of Los Angeles; for the female foot and the male jawline; for vintage clothes and cars and cigarettes. But the mood in this, his ninth feature, is for the most part affectionate rather than obsessive.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/24/arts/24onceupon1/24onceupon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/24/arts/24onceupon1/24onceupon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/24/arts/24onceupon1/24onceupon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 's