unt': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/6d1535f5-fd5a-5301-afd6-4a90b3d7d46a'}, {'abstract': 'In his latest Graphic Content column Ed Park looks at Daria Tessler’s “Cult of the Ibis” and “Tumult,” by John Harris Dunning and Michael Kennedy — two surreal adventures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/25/books/review/daria-tessler-cult-of-the-ibis-john-harris-dunning-michael-kennedy-tumult.html', 'snippet': 'In his latest Graphic Content column Ed Park looks at Daria Tessler’s “Cult of the Ibis” and “Tumult,” by John Harris Dunning and Michael Kennedy — two surreal adventures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some graphic novels are so visually stunning that you can readily imagine print dealers of the future slicing out pages for individual sale, as riveting out of context as extracts from a 19th-century field guide. Daria Tessler’s exquisite CULT OF THE IBIS (Fantagraphics Underground, $29.99) falls into this category, a tour de force in black and white. Nearly every picture balances a sense of wonder and menace — stopping you in your tracks even as the plot races ahead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/23/books/review/Tessler2/Tessler2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/23/books/review/Tessler2/Tessler2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/23/books/review/Tessler2/Tessler2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 496, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/23/books/review/Tessler2/Tessler2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 496, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ra