': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-26T04:33:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Zhong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zhong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9d85c8d-27b6-5bdd-a114-7fb4cf911167', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9d85c8d-27b6-5bdd-a114-7fb4cf911167'}, {'abstract': 'A streaming comic book adaptation gives fresh meaning to the word “antihero.” And rent “Dumbo” to catch up with recent Disney remakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/26/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday-the-boys-and-dumbo.html', 'snippet': 'A streaming comic book adaptation gives fresh meaning to the word “antihero.” And rent “Dumbo” to catch up with recent Disney remakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BOYS Stream on Amazon. A trailer for this streaming series adaptation of comic books by Garth Ennis and Darick Robertson features eye gouging, machine guns, lots of swearing and what appears to be a baby that fires lasers from its face. And that trailer is only about two minutes long. The thrust of the series — whose showrunner is the “Supernatural” creator Eric Kripke — is something like a dirty, topsy-turvy take on “The Avengers,” set in a world where badly behaving celebrity superheroes abuse their powers. Karl Urban plays Billy Butcher, the leader of a squad assembled to keep the superheroes’ misbehavior in check. That turns out to be a bit like employing hyenas to babysit coyotes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/26/arts/26tvcol-theboys/merlin_158182215_cead238e-b610-475e-b3c3-67b2720c64b9-articleLarge.jpg