'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Impersonators and Impressionists (Entertainment)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Weekly (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FX (TV Network)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hulu.com', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-26T08:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Universal', 'section_name': 'The Weekly', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9e23ba5-a9bf-5b97-b91f-ff321b4f1fbe', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9e23ba5-a9bf-5b97-b91f-ff321b4f1fbe'}, {'abstract': 'At the end of her sophomore year in college, Mary Beth Keane’s writing teacher gave her a summer reading list that changed her life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/26/books/review/mary-beth-keane-reading-list-mary-gordon.html', 'snippet': 'At the end of her sophomore year in college, Mary Beth Keane’s writing teacher gave her a summer reading list that changed her life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a kid in the late 1980s, the reading material in my house consisted of: our local newspaper, an Irish newspaper and Reader’s Digest, which came only once a month. My favorite section in Reader’s Digest was called “Drama in Real Life” and featured a true story about a life-threatening experience. Once, a babysitter asked me where my younger sisters’ bedtime books were kept. I told her there weren’t any books, but she could try reading “Drama in Real Life” to them if she wanted. On that occasion, the story was about a man who was cutting down trees on his property and somehow touched his chain saw to a steel wire. The saw got stripped of its blades and tore him up like shrapnel. I don’t remember how he survived, but I do remember that my sisters were riveted. What power, I thought, to be able to bring people to the edge of their seats, impatient to know what would happen next.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Keane/merlin_158305254_b7d7075b-867a-4616-8fdd-a6e5ff144314-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She Grew Up in a House Without Books. A Teacher Helped Her Realize She Could Write One Herself.', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Reading List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Mary (1949- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-26T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Beth Keane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Beth', 'lastname': 'Keane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5656b2d7-b389-5873-b246-03642a805ab5', 'word_count': 1515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5656b2d7-b389-5873-b246-03642a805ab5'}, {'abstract': 'Lori Gottlieb analyzes the benefits — and side effects — of bibliotherapy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/26/books/review/can-a-book-cure-mental-illness.html', 'snippet': 'Lori Gottlieb analyzes the benefits — and side effects — of bibliotherapy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first book I brought up to one of my therapy patients was Mary Karr’s “The Liars’ Club.” A young woman had been telling me about how ashamed she was of her parents, how she hid her family history from friends and romantic partners because, she reasoned, if she were to tell anyone the truth of her upbringing, nobody would understand.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/merlin_158620506_42745108-f140-4285-8d77-9d2b4d1437dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/merlin_158620506_42745108-f140-4285-8d77-9d2b4d1437dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/merlin_158620506_42745108-f140-4285-8d77-9d2b4d1437dc-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/merlin_158620506_42745108-f140-4285-8d77-9d2b4d1437dc-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1155, 'width': 1155, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/11Gottleib-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/11Gottleib-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/books/review/11Gottleib/11Gottleib-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Therapist’s Prescription? The Right Book.', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bibliotherapy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-26T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Gottlieb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gottlieb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb974fa3-77e6-5ac0-981b-69b76c13c0d7', 'word_count': 1385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb974fa3-77e6-5ac0-981b-69b76c13c0d7'}, {'abstract': 'From a cancer risk perspective, the American Institute for Cancer Research considers smoked and cured fish in the same category as processed meats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/26/well/eat/does-lox-and-other-smoked-fish-increase-cancer-risk.html', 'snippet': 'From a cancer risk perspective, the American Institute for Cancer Research considers smoked and cured fish in the same category as processed meats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Does eating smoked fish, such as smoked salmon or whitefish, increase the risk of colorectal cancer or other cancers, the way processed and deli meats do?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/30/well/ask-well-lox/ask-well-lox-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/30/well/ask-well-lox/ask-well-lox-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/30/well/ask-well-lox/a