c4dc59-13ba-5056-8a28-5e7514a75269', 'word_count': 1649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03c4dc59-13ba-5056-8a28-5e7514a75269'}, {'abstract': 'Karin Slaughter killed a character with antifreeze; Peter Swanson used cashews and a missing EpiPen. Welcome to the devious minds of thriller writers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/26/books/lee-child-ruth-ware-my-favorite-murder.html', 'snippet': 'Karin Slaughter killed a character with antifreeze; Peter Swanson used cashews and a missing EpiPen. Welcome to the devious minds of thriller writers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More women than men choose to kill people with poison, but one of my male characters in “Strawberry Yellow” uses ordinary rhubarb to murder his nemesis. It actually makes perfect sense as he’s a farmer and the mystery takes place in the salad bowl of agricultural California. The leaves of rhubarb contain a high level of oxalic acid, which can cause kidney failure. Boil the leaves and mix them together with chopped rhubarb and strawberries to make the most beautiful and deadly pie.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/28/books/review/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/28/books/review/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/28/books/review/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/28/books/review/28Tarng-Favorite-Murder/28Ta