d', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7cb7274-b2c5-5f6d-a735-fe37c3dcb151', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7cb7274-b2c5-5f6d-a735-fe37c3dcb151'}, {'abstract': 'The new cups from Welcome Industries have different shapes for cooks to quickly grab the right measure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/29/dining/visual-measuring-cups.html', 'snippet': 'The new cups from Welcome Industries have different shapes for cooks to quickly grab the right measure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pam Daniels, an industrial designer who founded Welcome Industries in Chicago with Brandon Williams, created this patented interpretation of an everyday set of measuring cups. The shape indicates each one’s size, making them as useful for adult cooks as they would be for a child learning to bake. They’re clear, unlike most measuring cups for dry ingredients and are dishwasher safe.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/31/dining/29burner5/merlin_158490111_7ff111b8-32f9-424f-99a5-2f984b9ed23e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/31/dining/29burner5/merlin_158490111_7ff111b8-32f9-424f-99a5-2f984b9ed23e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/31/dining/29burner5/merlin_158490111_7ff111b8-32f9-424f-99a5-2f984b9ed23e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capt