ype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04newnote/04newnote-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04newnote/04newnote-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04newnote/04newnote-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy Books, From Italian History to Asian Politics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-30T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5b8cd20-703b-54bc-b4b1-cc0ea7dd8ebf', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5b8cd20-703b-54bc-b4b1-cc0ea7dd8ebf'}, {'abstract': '“Gods of the Upper Air,” by Charles King, tells the story of the anthropologist Franz Boas and those he influenced, including Margaret Mead and Zora Neale Hurston.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/30/books/review-gods-of-upper-air-charles-king.html', 'snippet': '“Gods of the Upper Air,” by Charles King, tells the story of the anthropologist Franz Boas and those he influenced, including Margaret Mead and Zora Neale Hurston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the 1930s, the New York-based anthropologist Franz Boas grew increasingly worried about events in his native Germany. He was in his 70s, and close to retiring from Columbia University, where he taught his students to reject the junk science underpinning the country’s restrictive immigration laws, colonial expansion and Jim Crow. Born into a Jewish burgher family, Boas was horrified to see how the Nazis took inspiration from Americans’ pathbreaking work in eugenics and state-sanctioned bigotry. He started to put the word “race” in scare quotes, calling it a “dangerous fiction.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 888}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1629, 'width': 1101, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/01/books/01bookking1/01bookking1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Group of Heretical Thinkers Chipped Away at the Idea of ‘Us’ and ‘Them’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Defusing the Idea Of ‘Us’ and ‘Them’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gods of the Upper Air: How a Circle of Renegade Anthropologists Reinvented Race, Sex, and Gender in the Twentieth Century (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Charles (1967- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY AND ANTHROPOLOGY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-30T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/457002bc-682a-5562-a535-c88d2380b547', 'word_count': 1051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/457002bc-682a-5562-a535-c88d2380b547'}, {'abstract': 'Give the camera someone else’s face — or obscure your own. Strive for asymmetry. Make your head unheadlike.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/30/magazine/how-to-thwart-facial-recognition.html', 'snippet': 'Give the camera someone else’s face — or obscure your own. Strive for asymmetry. Make your head unheadlike.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Why not give the camera what it wants, which is a face?” says Leonardo Selvaggio, an interdisciplinary artist. Just don’t give it your face. To enable people to obfuscate facial-recognition software programs, Selvaggio, who is 34 and white, made available 3-D, photo-realistic prosthetic masks of his own face to anyone who wants one. He tested the masks by asking people connected to him on Facebook to upload pictures of themselves in the prosthetic: It didn’t matter if they were skinny women or barrel-chested men; short or tall; black, brown, Asian or white — the social network’s facial-recognition software recognized them as Selvaggio. “There’s nothing more invisible to surveillance and security technology than a white man,” he says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/magazine/04mag-tip-image1/04mag-tip-image1-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 351, 'width': 342, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/magazine/04mag-tip-image1/04mag-tip-image1-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 351, 'width': 342, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/magazine/04mag-tip-image1/04mag-tip-image1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/04/magazine/04mag-tip-image1/04mag-tip-image1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/magazine/04mag-tip-image1/04mag-tip-image1-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Thwart Facial Recognition', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Thwart Facial Recognition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Face', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Facial Recognition Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Masks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-07-30T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79031f7b-da2b-5179-9fcd-ba9843472f2c', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79031f7b-da2b-5179-9fcd-ba9843472f2c'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether to rat out a colleague who makes up for their lack of competence by using you as a crutch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/30/magazine/workplace-incompetence-ethics.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether to rat out a colleague who makes up for their lack of competence by using you as a crutch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I work at a small nonprofit as the assistant to the executive director. Very shortly after I was hired, a new writer was also hired. Let’s call her Carrie. Carrie did not have experience in writing the particular kinds of proposals our nonprofit requires, but the executive director thought she was a good fit for the position. There was a rough start to her tenure, as a lot of deadlines came up quickly and she was doing a lot of things last minute. Her writing was not the best during this period, but both the director and I chalked it up to her rush in trying to hit the deadlines. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/04mag-ethicist-image1/04mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v34.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/04mag-ethicist-image1/04mag-ethicist-imag