'By Sebastian Modak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebastian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Modak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e944057-2d98-57ad-956a-aeb3f7394fb9', 'word_count': 1588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e944057-2d98-57ad-956a-aeb3f7394fb9'}, {'abstract': 'The Wayuu have long lived between Venezuela and Colombia. But as Venezuela collapses, many are seeking refuge with their Colombian kin, straining resources — and relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/07/30/world/americas/venezuela-indigenous-wayuu.html', 'snippet': 'The Wayuu have long lived between Venezuela and Colombia. But as Venezuela collapses, many are seeking refuge with their Colombian kin, straining resources — and relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARENSTU, Colombia — They had lived off the land for hundreds of years, before Venezuela or Colombia had even been founded. The Wayuu, an indigenous group of shepherds in South America, had survived war, upheaval, revolution and even being separated from one another by the creation of national borders.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/03/world/xxvenez-wayuu-promo/xxvenez-wayuu-promo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/07/03/world/xxvenez-wayuu-promo/xxvenez-wayuu-promo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/07/03/world/xxvenez-wayuu-promo/xxvenez-wayuu-promo-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'